  "usage_note": "The comparative and superlative are **worse** and **worst**: *he played worse than last week*. Before a past participle, **badly** comes first: *badly hurt*, *badly paid*. In writing, use **badly**, not **bad**, after an action verb.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "She speaks English very bad.",
      "correct": "She speaks English very badly.",
      "note": "**Bad** is an adjective; after an action verb use the adverb **badly**."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "He played more badly than last week.",
      "correct": "He played worse than last week.",
      "note": "The comparative of **badly** is **worse**."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "**Badly** can mean *not well* (*sing badly*) or *very much* (*badly need*); many languages use unrelated words for these. *Badly hurt* means seriously hurt, not hurt in a careless way.",
    "grammar": "The comparative and superlative are **worse** and **worst**, the same forms as for **bad**.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Two syllables with the stress on the first: *BAD-lee*. The **a** is the open sound of **cat**."
